● I added t/lm.R.scipy.t — 1558 tests cross-validating lm() against R 4.6.1
  stats::lm and statsmodels 0.14.6/scipy 1.17.1. Full suite: 110 files, 10731
  tests, all pass.

  What it covers

  30 generated cases, each checking rank, df.residual, rows-kept, rss, R²,
  adj-R², the F triple, f.pvalue, and per-term Estimate / Std. Error / t value /
  Pr(>|t|), plus sampled fitted values and residuals. Data: mtcars, iris,
  ToothGrowth, warpbreaks, airquality, the shipped t/bodyfat.csv, and synthetics
  for an exact fit, a rank-deficient design, hand-placed NAs, p≈n, and
  predictors spanning 1e-4…1e6. Paths exercised: *, :, I(x^2), -1/+0, .
  expansion, dotted column names, factors, NA dropping, aliasing. Plus
  hand-written blocks for ^ collapsing, duplicate terms, intercept-only (no F
  stat), HoA/HoH/AoH agreement, constant-column aliasing, and xlevels ordering.

  lm() matches both references everywhere it's defined. Worst deviation in the
  file is 1.4e-8 relative, on the 15-predictor BodyFat ~ . — three orders inside
  the eps·κ(X'X) bound that forming X'X imposes.

  Three real divergences

  1. Factor with no intercept — genuine bug. len ~ supp - 1: with no intercept
  there's no baseline, so R and statsmodels keep all levels. lm() drops the
  reference level anyway, fitting len ~ suppVC - 1 — forcing every OJ row to a
  fitted value of 0. rss 16056 vs R's 3247, R² 0.35 vs 0.87. Not a precision
  issue; a wrong model.

  2. Interactions with a factor — unsupported, misleading error. len ~ dose * 
  supp dies with 0 degrees of freedom (too many NAs or parameters > 
  observations). LikeR.xs:15563 asks is_column_categorical for a column
  literally named dose:supp, gets FALSE, so evaluate_term returns NaN for every
  row and all rows drop. Affects factor×numeric and factor×factor; glm() has the
  same gap. Notably read.me.pod:9511 documents predict() as handling GroupB:x
  coefficients — so predict() consumes names lm() can't produce.

  Both are marked TODO with R's expected values inline, so they pass unchanged
  once implemented.

  3. Mixed-case level ordering — not a bug. lm() sorts levels with strcmp (byte
  order), matching patsy/pandas; R uses locale collation, so on c("b","A","a") R
  takes a as reference and lm() takes A. Same fit, different parameterisation —
  pinned to the statsmodels answer with R's noted.

  Also documented (not TODO, it's obscure): wt:wt is built as wt², where R's
  formula algebra collapses a:a to a.

  Notes on method

  Tolerances are per-case, derived from measured deviation × 200, floored at
  1e-11 — the header carries an auto-generated table of all 21 cases above the
  floor with the quantity that deviates and its eps·κ bound, so the prose can't
  drift. Two error sources are separated: the linear solve (dominates the
  bodyfat models) and the p-value tail functions (why some well-conditioned
  cases sit above eps·κ). Two cases compare against R only, because statsmodels'
  pinv gives a valid but different rank-deficient parameterisation.

  I mutation-tested the file: perturbing each of 15 reported quantities by 1e-7
  produces 2–224 failures, so nothing is vacuously asserted.

  Generator scripts (R + statsmodels + Perl dumpers, three-way differ, .t
  emitter) are in /tmp/claude-1000/-home-con-Scripts-stats/050e400d-a9c8-4e6f-b0
  de-143aecd7fa84/scratchpad/lmref/. Want me to commit those alongside the other
  .R reference scripts, or fix the no-intercept factor bug?

