● Final three-way comparison for the record, and a re-run of the mutation check
  on the expanded test file:

  Ran 2 shell commands (ctrl+o to expand)

● Fixed all three genuine bugs, plus a fourth in predict that the first fix
  exposed. Full suite: 110 files, 11438 tests, all pass.

  The fixes

  The root cause was that lm and glm each carried a copy of design-matrix
  construction that expanded one term at a time and always dropped a factor's
  first level. I replaced both copies with a shared builder (LikeR.xs:585-900)
  that decides coding per component using R's margin rule: a factor is coded by 
  contrasts when the term with that factor removed is itself in the model, and 
  in full when it is not — with the empty margin (the intercept) consumable only
  once, which is what stops y ~ a + b - 1 from coding both factors in full and
  going rank-deficient.

  1. Factor in a no-intercept model lost a level. len ~ supp - 1 fitted len ~ 
  suppVC - 1, forcing every OJ row to a fitted value of 0 — rss 16056 vs R's
  3247, R² 0.35 vs 0.87. Now one column per group, each coefficient that group's
  mean.
  2. Interactions with a factor died. dose:supp was looked up as a single column
  of that literal name, evaluated to NaN for every row, dropped every row, then
  died with 0 degrees of freedom. Now expands to products of indicator columns.
  3. a*b*c expanded only its first * (README.md listed this under "known
  limitations"). Crossing is associative, so it now yields all 2ⁿ−1 subsets
  ordered by degree. Crossing >16 columns croaks rather than expanding.
  4. predict scored reference-level rows as 0 — it registered dummies from
  levels[1..] only, correct for contrast coding but not for the full coding fix
  1 introduced. Exposed by my own test, not reported earlier.

  Verification

  lm and glm now match R 4.6.1 exactly — rank, df, rss, term names,
  coefficients, standard errors, and which column gets aliased — across every
  case I could construct: factor×numeric, factor×factor, bare interactions (full
  cross-classification), three-way crossing, I(x^2)*g, no-intercept variants, y
  ~ . with factors, and NA-in-factor row dropping. glm agrees for gaussian and
  poisson.

  No case regressed: I measured worst deviation from R for all 44 cases before
  and after — nothing got worse, the two bugs went from structurally wrong to
  ~1e-13. No SV leaks (Test::LeakTrace), and RSS is flat across 144k fits, so no
  malloc leak either.

  Tests: t/lm.R.scipy.t grew to 2218 (the two TODO blocks became real cases,
  plus 13 new cases and a coding: section pinning column sets); added glm and
  predict regression blocks. Re-ran the mutation check — all 16 reported
  quantities still load-bearing.

  Two things to flag

  - The remaining needs attention entries are all understood non-issues: the
  ill-conditioned bodyfat pair (within the eps·κ(X'X) bound), zero-residual
  noise, near-zero-residual cancellation, statsmodels' pinv convention on
  rank-deficient fits, and the two by-design divergences. For no-intercept
  models statsmodels reports a centered R² (it sees the full dummy columns
  summing to 1 and sets k_constant=1); lm matches R's uncentered value to the
  last digit.
  - Pre-existing, not fixed: glm's poisson standard errors differ from R at
  ~5e-8 relative while coefficients agree to 12 digits — the information matrix
  is built from the final IRLS weights and the deviance-based stop (epsilon = 
  1e-8) leaves them a hair from R's. It shows on a plain y ~ x + z poisson fit
  too, so it's the convergence criterion, not the design matrix. I bounded it at
  1e-5 in the test with that rationale rather than silently tightening or
  chasing it. Worth a look separately if you want R agreement on glm SEs.

  I also updated the ## lm docs and the 0.291 changelog in README.md and
  regenerated the POD — including the three deliberate R divergences (byte-order
  level sorting, wt:wt as a square, single-level factors). I initially edited
  the "known limitations" note inside the ## 0.16 release-notes section and
  reverted that: it correctly described the state at that release.

✻ Worked for 34m 37s

