● Final confirmation — the full glm battery against R, and the whole suite:

  Ran 1 shell command (ctrl+o to expand)

● Fixed. The cause wasn't where I'd guessed — it was a step-halving bug, not the
  information matrix.

  Diagnosis

  glm's standard errors come from the information matrix of the penultimate IRLS
  iterate, and so do R's: summary.glm inverts the QR that glm.fit kept from its
  last weighted least-squares call, so f$weights sits one step behind
  f$fitted.values. I confirmed this in R — rebuilding the SEs from f$weights
  reproduces summary.glm to 1.9e-14, while rebuilding from the weights at the
  converged β misses by 5.4e-8.

  Agreeing with R therefore requires stopping on the same iteration. glm was
  taking 7 where R takes 4, because of this at LikeR.xs:11964:

  if (is_gaussian || deviance_new <= deviance_old + 1e-7 ||
  !isfinite(deviance_new))
          continue;                    /* accept */
  boundary = TRUE;                     /* else halve the step */

  Any rise in deviance was read as divergence. But the standard IRLS start puts
  mu at y + 0.1 — essentially on the data — so the initial deviance is near zero
  and the first real step must raise it. On the nine-point poisson fit the
  sequence is 0.016 → 1.54 → 1.510 → 1.5101827, so step one was halved ten times
  over, crippling it. R halves only when the deviance is non-finite. The extra
  iterations still reached the same MLE, which is why the symptom showed up only
  in the standard errors — coefficients agreeing to 12 digits while SEs were
  5e-8 to 2e-5 out was the tell.

  That same condition also had isfinite on the accepting side, so a genuinely
  divergent step producing a NaN deviance was kept rather than truncated. Now
  inverted to match R.

  Result

  ┌────────────────────┬─────────────────┬───────────────────┐
  │                    │ SE vs R before  │       after       │
  ├────────────────────┼─────────────────┼───────────────────┤
  │ poisson (6 models) │ 5.2e-8 … 2.1e-5 │ 1.8e-15 … 7.3e-14 │
  ├────────────────────┼─────────────────┼───────────────────┤
  │ binomial           │ 5.7e-7, 1.9e-6  │ 1.7e-14, 1.8e-11  │
  ├────────────────────┼─────────────────┼───────────────────┤
  │ gaussian           │ 6.3e-15         │ unchanged         │
  └────────────────────┴─────────────────┴───────────────────┘

  Gaussian is untouched by construction — its weights are all 1, so the matrix
  is X'X either way, and the numbers are bit-identical.

  glm now takes R's exact iteration count (4 for that poisson fit, 8 for am ~ wt
  + hp binomial), and on a perfectly separable logistic fit matches R's
  iter=25, converged=FALSE, boundary=FALSE.

  One characterized residual: bin_mt_int (am ~ wt * hp) stays at 1.9e-9. That's
  entirely the varmu floor of 1e-10 — a guard against dividing by an underflowed
  variance. I verified the attribution by applying that same floor to R's own
  weights, which reproduces 1.941572e-9 exactly. It bites on 3 of 32
  observations whose fitted probabilities are within 1e-12 of 0 or 1, in a fit
  where R itself warns "fitted probabilities numerically 0 or 1", and
  κ(X'WX)=1.15e8 permits 2.6e-8 anyway. I left the guard alone and documented it
  rather than loosening a numerical safeguard unasked.

  Test changes

  - t/glm.t: tightened the poisson SE bound from the 1e-5 I'd set earlier to
  1e-9, added SEs to the no-intercept poisson case, and added a regression block
  pinning iter == 4 (poisson) and iter == 8 (binomial) with R's 17-digit
  coefficients and SEs — plus an assertion that SE accuracy tracks coefficient
  accuracy, since the divergence between the two was the original signature.
  - t/model_pvalue_tails.t: six expected_p values were stale. That file
  documents expected_p as "norm.sf evaluated at THIS module's own statistic",
  and z moved (toward R), so I regenerated them from scipy at the new z. Its p 
  vs R checks passed throughout and got tighter — z now matches R to 5.4e-16.

  Full suite: 110 files, 11460 tests, all pass. No SV leaks across
  poisson/binomial/negbin/separable fits, and RSS is flat over 64k glm fits.
  Changelog and POD updated.

  Unrelated and not touched: MASS::glm.nb agreement on the negative-binomial
  path is ~1e-6 on theta and coefficients — that's the theta-ML outer loop's own
  1e-7 loglik tolerance, a separate convergence question from this one.

