● Fixed. It turned out to be two distinct defects, and MASS's algorithm is
  subtler than "maximise the likelihood over theta".

  Diagnosis

  glm.nb alternates — an IRLS fit at the current theta, then a fresh ML estimate
  of theta at the current means — and which fit it lands on depends entirely on
  the schedule. Four things about that schedule were wrong:

  1. The stopping rule. glm used a relative test on the log-likelihood alone,
  |dll| < 1e-7·(|ll|+0.1). MASS requires (|dLm|/d1 + |dtheta|) < 1e-8 with d1 = 
  sqrt(2·max(1, df.residual)) from its Poisson pass — theta itself must have
  settled, not just the likelihood. The old test fired ~2e-5 of log-likelihood
  early. This was the dominant cause.
  2. The first pass must be a genuine Poisson fit, not a negative-binomial fit
  at theta = 1e6. It supplies both the first theta and d1.
  3. Later passes must be warm-started from the previous means (etastart = 
  log(mu)), not cold-started.
  4. Theta is re-estimated at the means each pass started from — glm.nb calls
  theta.ml(Y, mu) and only then reassigns mu <- fit$fitted.values. That lag is
  easy to miss and moves theta in the eighth digit. theta.ml now also uses
  MASS's own absolute step tolerance of .Machine$double.eps^0.25.

  Results, over 18 fits spanning theta 0.41 → 69,000

  ┌─────────────────┬────────┬─────────┐
  │                 │ before │  after  │
  ├─────────────────┼────────┼─────────┤
  │ theta           │ 8e-7   │ 3.4e-9  │
  ├─────────────────┼────────┼─────────┤
  │ coefficients    │ 8e-6   │ 5.8e-9  │
  ├─────────────────┼────────┼─────────┤
  │ standard errors │ 3e-6   │ 8.4e-10 │
  ├─────────────────┼────────┼─────────┤
  │ deviance        │ 6e-7   │ 1.3e-9  │
  └─────────────────┴────────┴─────────┘

  A second defect this surfaced

  While testing I found that a negbin fit with theta supplied started from
  Poisson's mustart (y + 0.1), where R's negative.binomial()$initialize uses y +
  (y == 0)/6. Different starting values walk different iterates, and since
  standard errors come from the penultimate one, SEs were 6e-7 out while
  coefficients agreed to 1e-9 — the same signature as the poisson bug. Those 
  fits now match R to 2e-15.

  Two things worth knowing

  The near-Poisson case is not fixable and shouldn't be. For Poisson-drawn
  counts, theta agrees only to ~4e-6 relative. But theta is unidentified there:
  its own standard error is 903,511 against an estimate of 58,547, a 10% change
  in theta moves the log-likelihood by 7e-5, and glm.nb's theta.ml reports
  "iteration limit reached" — MASS's own value is an unconverged iterate. The
  coefficients still agree to 1.6e-10. I've documented it rather than chasing
  it.

  A dispersion convention, verified not assumed. My first cross-check against
  glm(family = negative.binomial(theta)) showed a 4% SE gap. That's because
  summary.glm, handed a negative.binomial family, estimates the dispersion
  (1.0839 on that data) and scales SEs by its square root, whereas
  glm.nb/summary.negbin fix it at 1. glm follows the glm.nb convention; against
  summary(fit, dispersion = 1) it agrees to 2e-15. I noted this in the test and
  the docs since it's a live trap for anyone comparing.

  I also had to correct one of my own test assertions: I'd asserted that
  refitting at the reported theta reproduces the alternation's coefficients. It
  doesn't — the returned fit predates the final theta update — and I confirmed
  MASS shows the same 5.5e-9 gap, so the test now pins that behaviour instead.

  Tests

  t/glm_families.t: existing negbin reference values replaced with 17-digit MASS
  values and tolerances tightened from 1e-3…1e-5 to 1e-11…1e-8; new blocks for
  moderate overdispersion, the near-Poisson regime, and the fixed-theta path.

  Full suite: 110 files, 11485 tests, all pass. No SV leaks across
  ML/fixed-theta/croak paths, RSS flat over 12k negbin fits (I moved the new
  allocation to first use so a bad-response croak can't leak it). Poisson,
  binomial and gaussian are unchanged from the previous fix — re-verified at
  5e-12 / 1.9e-9 / 1.6e-14. Changelog and POD updated.

✻ Brewed for 20m 54s

※ recap: Goal was validating Stats::LikeR's lm against R and scipy; that grew into fixing four bugs in lm, glm, and predict, all now done with 11485 tests passing. Nothing is pending, so the next step is yours: review or commit. (disable recaps in /config)